/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A carburettor speeds the incoming air through a narrow throat, the venturi; the pressure drop there draws fuel through a fixed jet.
   - Electronic control of carburetion was the stop-gap: a solenoid valve bled air into the carburettor fuel circuits, moved by the ECU from the oxygen-sensor signal.
   - Petrol injection replaced the jet with a solenoid valve opened for a computed time.
   - Port injectors can fire all at once once per revolution (simultaneous), in two groups (group), or each just before its own intake stroke (sequential, which needs a camshaft sensor to know the cylinder).
   - Inside the injector a coil pulls an armature and needle off its seat against a spring; fuel at rail pressure sprays through the nozzle.
   - The speed-density calculation turns manifold pressure into an open time in four steps.
   - Here ηv is volumetric efficiency, the air actually trapped divided by the swept volume's worth at manifold density, read from a map (typically 0.8 to 0.9 at full load; tuned intakes can push it slightly above 1).
   - The base pulse is then corrected: extra fuel when the coolant is cold, a short extra pulse when the throttle opens suddenly (fuel wets the port walls), and a longer dead time at low battery voltage.
   - The switching oxygen sensor says only rich (high voltage) or lean (low voltage), not by how much.
   - Short-term fuel trim (STFT) is that live correction, a percentage added to or taken from the base pulse.
*/
