/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The coil stores energy only while its primary circuit is switched on.
   - Dwell time td is how long, in milliseconds, the ECU keeps the primary on before the break.
   - Dwell angle θd is the same interval in crank degrees.
   - θd is the dwell angle in crank degrees; N is engine speed in rpm; td is the dwell time in ms; 0.006 = 360 ÷ 60 000.
   - At 3000 rpm one millisecond is 18 degrees, so 3.0 ms is 54 degrees.
   - The mixture takes a few milliseconds to burn.
   - More advance starts the burn earlier and moves the pressure peak towards TDC, so torque rises, until a point past which it falls again because the piston works against rising pressure.
   - Knock is the unburnt end gas ahead of the flame igniting by itself, giving a sharp pressure spike that rings the block and, if it lasts, cracks pistons.
   - The ECU fires at θspark = min(θMBT, θknock − Δ), all in degrees BTDC, where Δ is a 1 to 3 degree safety margin.
   - Base advance comes from a two-dimensional map with engine speed on one axis and load (air per cycle, from the air-flow or manifold-pressure sensor) on the other: the same kind of map and interpolation as the fuel table.
*/
